## Well-Designed Controller

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/orders")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class OrderController {

    private final OrderService orderService;      // Business logic here
    private final OrderMapper orderMapper;         // Mapping here

    // List with filtering and pagination
    @GetMapping
    public Page<OrderResponse> listOrders(
            @RequestParam(required = false) OrderStatus status,
            @RequestParam(required = false) @DateTimeFormat(iso = DateTimeFormat.ISO.DATE)
                LocalDate from,
            @PageableDefault(size = 20, sort = "createdAt", direction = Sort.Direction.DESC)
                Pageable pageable,
            @AuthenticationPrincipal UserDetails currentUser) {
        // Thin: build query, delegate, map result
        return orderService.findOrders(status, from, pageable, currentUser.getUsername())
            .map(orderMapper::toResponse);
    }

    // Get single resource
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderDetailResponse getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return orderMapper.toDetailResponse(orderService.findById(id));
    }

    // Create resource — return 201 with Location header
    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<OrderResponse> createOrder(
            @Valid @RequestBody CreateOrderRequest request,
            @AuthenticationPrincipal UserDetails currentUser) {
        Order order = orderService.createOrder(request, currentUser.getUsername());
        URI location = URI.create("/api/v1/orders/" + order.getId());
        return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(orderMapper.toResponse(order));
    }

    // Full update
    @PutMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderResponse updateOrder(
            @PathVariable Long id,
            @Valid @RequestBody UpdateOrderRequest request) {
        return orderMapper.toResponse(orderService.update(id, request));
    }

    // Partial update
    @PatchMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderResponse partialUpdate(
            @PathVariable Long id,
            @RequestBody @Valid PatchOrderRequest request) {
        return orderMapper.toResponse(orderService.partialUpdate(id, request));
    }

    // Delete — 204 No Content
    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)
    public void deleteOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
        orderService.delete(id);
    }

    // Action sub-resource
    @PostMapping("/{id}/cancel")
    public OrderResponse cancelOrder(
            @PathVariable Long id,
            @RequestBody(required = false) CancelOrderRequest request) {
        return orderMapper.toResponse(orderService.cancel(id, request));
    }

    // Custom header extraction
    @GetMapping("/export")
    public ResponseEntity<byte[]> exportOrders(
            @RequestHeader(value = "Accept-Language", defaultValue = "en") String lang) {
        byte[] data = orderService.exportOrReport(lang);
        return ResponseEntity.ok()
            .contentType(MediaType.valueOf("text/csv"))
            .header(HttpHeaders.CONTENT_DISPOSITION, "attachment; filename=orders.csv")
            .body(data);
    }
}
```

## ResponseEntity vs @ResponseStatus

```java
// @ResponseStatus: static status code for method
@GetMapping("/{id}")
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.OK)  // Optional — 200 is default for GET
public OrderResponse getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return orderMapper.toResponse(orderService.findById(id));
}

@DeleteMapping("/{id}")
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)  // 204 — no body returned
public void delete(@PathVariable Long id) {
    orderService.delete(id);
}

// ResponseEntity: dynamic status, headers, or conditional responses
@GetMapping("/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<OrderResponse> getOrder(
        @PathVariable Long id,
        @RequestHeader(value = "If-None-Match", required = false) String ifNoneMatch) {
    Order order = orderService.findById(id);
    String etag = "\"" + order.getVersion() + "\"";

    if (etag.equals(ifNoneMatch)) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_MODIFIED).build();
    }

    return ResponseEntity.ok()
        .eTag(etag)
        .cacheControl(CacheControl.maxAge(Duration.ofMinutes(5)))
        .body(orderMapper.toResponse(order));
}
```

## @ModelAttribute and Form Data

```java
// For form data (application/x-www-form-urlencoded):
@PostMapping(value = "/submit", consumes = MediaType.APPLICATION_FORM_URLENCODED_VALUE)
public ResponseEntity<Void> submitForm(@ModelAttribute @Valid OrderFormRequest form) {
    orderService.processForm(form);
    return ResponseEntity.ok().build();
}

// @ModelAttribute on @Controller (not @RestController):
@ModelAttribute("categories")
public List<Category> categories() {
    return categoryService.findAll();  // Added to model for all handler methods
}
```

## Production Reality

Keep controllers focused on HTTP concerns: method selection, path, request extraction, response status, headers. The moment you start writing `if (user.isAdmin() && order.getStatus() == PENDING && order.getTotal() > 1000)` in a controller, that logic belongs in the service. Controllers should be testable with `@WebMvcTest` and MockMvc without starting the full Spring context.

## CompletableFuture in Controller

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/reports")
public class ReportController {

    @Autowired
    private ReportService reportService;  // @Async methods

    // Async: thread released while report generates
    @GetMapping("/{type}")
    public CompletableFuture<Report> generateReport(@PathVariable String type) {
        return reportService.generateAsync(type);
        // HTTP thread: released immediately
        // Report thread: generates report in @Async thread pool
        // When future completes: response sent from its thread
    }

    // Error handling:
    @GetMapping("/{type}/with-error-handling")
    public CompletableFuture<ResponseEntity<Report>> generateWithErrors(@PathVariable String type) {
        return reportService.generateAsync(type)
            .thenApply(report -> ResponseEntity.ok(report))
            .exceptionally(ex -> ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR).build());
    }
}

@Service
public class ReportService {
    @Async("reportTaskExecutor")
    public CompletableFuture<Report> generateAsync(String type) {
        // This runs in the report thread pool — not the HTTP thread
        Report report = generate(type);  // Potentially slow operation
        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(report);
    }
}
```

## Server-Sent Events (SSE)

```java
@GetMapping(value = "/stream", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
public SseEmitter streamOrderUpdates() {
    SseEmitter emitter = new SseEmitter(30_000L);  // 30s timeout

    // Register with order update service:
    String subscriptionId = orderUpdateService.subscribe(event -> {
        try {
            emitter.send(SseEmitter.event()
                .id(String.valueOf(event.getId()))
                .name("order-update")
                .data(event, MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
            );
        } catch (IOException e) {
            emitter.completeWithError(e);
        }
    });

    emitter.onCompletion(() -> orderUpdateService.unsubscribe(subscriptionId));
    emitter.onTimeout(() -> orderUpdateService.unsubscribe(subscriptionId));
    emitter.onError(e -> orderUpdateService.unsubscribe(subscriptionId));

    return emitter;
}
```

## DeferredResult — External Callback

```java
// For results that come from outside (message queue, event, etc.):
@GetMapping("/poll/{jobId}")
public DeferredResult<JobResult> pollJobResult(@PathVariable String jobId) {
    DeferredResult<JobResult> deferredResult = new DeferredResult<>(30_000L);  // 30s timeout

    // Register callback — result set when job completes
    jobRegistry.registerCallback(jobId, result -> {
        deferredResult.setResult(result);
    });

    deferredResult.onTimeout(() -> {
        deferredResult.setErrorResult(
            ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.REQUEST_TIMEOUT)
                .body("Job not completed within timeout")
        );
    });

    return deferredResult;
    // HTTP thread: released immediately
    // When job completes: callback calls setResult() → response sent
}
```

## Production Reality

For most CRUD APIs, synchronous controllers are simpler and sufficient. Use async when: (1) the operation is genuinely CPU-heavy or IO-heavy and you expect high concurrency, (2) you're streaming large datasets, (3) you're implementing long-polling. Consider Spring WebFlux (reactive) for truly high-throughput streaming scenarios — it's designed for it, while Spring MVC async is an adapter over the servlet model.
